  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "Cameras captured a geometric tower thirteen thousand feet underwater. In 1964, an Antarctic research ship snapped a photo of something impossible in the pitch-black abyss. A slender pole stood in the mud with perfect, symmetrical crossbars.",
        "visual_query": "1960s antarctic ocean research ship",
        "visual_fallback": "deep dark ocean water"
      },
      {
        "narration": "It looked like a modern broadcast antenna sitting where no human could walk. UFO researchers seized on the photo, insisting it was an alien relay probe or a secret military device.",
        "visual_query": "deep sea underwater ocean abyss mud",
        "visual_fallback": "dark ocean floor"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Could a natural organism really form such flawless, mechanical right angles? Conspiracy theorists argued it was impossible, especially when naval experts confirmed no military tech was ever dropped there.",
        "visual_query": "antarctic ocean water ice shelf",
        "visual_fallback": "freezing dark ocean"
      },
      {
        "narration": "But the mystery vanished when marine biologists matched the photograph to drawings from an 1888 deep-sea study. The object wasn't metal or machinery at all\u2014it was living tissue.",
        "visual_query": "marine biologist deep ocean laboratory",
        "visual_fallback": "scientist with microscope"
      },
      {
        "narration": "It was Cladorhiza concrescens, a bizarre carnivorous ocean sponge that grows symmetrical branches to snag prey in pitch-black currents. Nature had simply designed its own underwater antenna.",
        "visual_query": "deep sea carnivorous sponge species",
        "visual_fallback": "strange deep ocean creature"
      }
    ],
